2 CONT '''Catherine I (Russian: Екатерина I Алексеевна; Yekaterina I Alekseyevna''' (born Marta Helena Skowrońska, Latvian: Marta Elena Skavronska, later Marfa Samuilovna Skavronskaya)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/tieto/4/0279.html
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://books.google.fi/books?id=AJQWCgAAQBAJ&pg=PA13&lpg=PA13&dq=Samuil+Skavronski&source=bl&ots=K1OHuMbZNf&sig=GJf6AmRPXml6rOjecZQ2sGVX_Nw&hl=fi&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjaz5O-uaLVAhXJF5oKHTiFDGs4ChDoAQgeMAA#v=onepage&q=Samuil%20Skavron
2 CONC ski&f=false Marta of Muscovy: The Fabulous Life of Russia's First Empress...  (FreeGoogleE-book) ]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Links'''
2 CONT *[http://thepeerage.com/p10195.htm#i101944 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/W/per_page.php?id=4652 Geneall]
2 CONT *''Empress of Russia''' Reign 1725-1727 '''Predecessor:'''  [http://www.geni.com/people/Peter-I/6000000005599137513 Peter I] '''Successor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Peter-II/6000000009534171762 Peter II]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_I_of_Russia Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.tartumaamuuseum.ee/?505 The real history of Rõngu Marta (in Estonian) - as she was born in Governorate of Livonia, in parrish Rõngu kihelkond (Kirchspiel Ringen) as serf's daughter, later czar's court rewrote her gene
2 CONC alogy to fit into blue-blooded]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The life of Catherine I was said by Voltaire to be nearly as extraordinary as Peter the Great himself. There are no documents that confirm the ascent of Catherine. The commonly accepted version is that Catherine was born in Ringen
2 CONC  (Rõngu), in present-day Estonia. At the time this area was the Swedish province of Livonia. Originally named 'Marta Skowrońska', she was the daughter of Samuel Skowroński, later Samuil Skavronsky, a Latvian peasant of Polish orig
2 CONC in, most likely a Catholic, and who was already a widower of one Dorothea Hann. Her mother has been listed on at least one site as Elisabeth Moritz, whom her father married at Jakobstadt in 1680. There is some speculation that her
2 CONC  parents were runaway serfs. Some sources state her father was a gravedigger. Samuil and her mother died of plague around 1684 or 1685, leaving five children. She was taken by an aunt who sent her to be raised by Ernst Glück, the 
2 CONC Lutheran pastor and educator who first translated the Bible into Latvian, in Marienburg. She was essentially a house servant. No effort was made to teach her to read and she remained illiterate throughout her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_I_of_Russia
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann Ernst Glück (Latvian: Ernsts Gliks; 10 November 1654 – 5 May 1705) was a German translator and Lutheran theologian active in Livonia, which is now in Latvia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Glück was born in Wettin as the son of a pastor. After attending the Latin school of Altenburg, he studied theology, rhetoric, philosophy, geometry, history, geography, and Latin at Wittenberg and Jena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Glück is renowned for translating the Holy Bible into the Latvian language, which he carried out in its entirety in Marienburg (Alūksne) in Livonia, in the building now the Alūksne Museum, established to honour his work. He also f
2 CONC ounded the first Latvian language schools in Livonia in 1683. He died in Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had four daughters, a son, and a foster-daughter Marta Skavronska who married Peter I and is mainly known as Catherine I. From 1725 until 1727 she was empress of Russian Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernst_Gl%C3%BCck
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
