2 CONT Catherine I, real name Marta Skavronskaya (1682?-1727), empress of Russia  (1725-27). Of peasant origin, she was born in Jakobstadt (now Jèkabpils,  Latvia) but was orphaned early in life and reared by a pastor in  Marienburg (now
2 CONC  Malbork, Poland). When the Russians captured Marienburg in  1702, she was taken prisoner by the Russian commander, who sold her to  Prince Aleksandr Menshikov (1673-1729), a close adviser of Peter the  Great. She soon became Pete
2 CONC r's mistress and most influential counselor.  Peter, who had divorced his first wife in 1699, married Catherine in 1712.  After his son Alexis (1690-1718) died, Peter issued an ukaz (imperial  order) declaring his right to name hi
2 CONC s own successor; he died in 1725  without doing so. Catherine, however, had been crowned empress-consort in  1724, and on Peter's death she was proclaimed his successor; the claims of  Alexis's son (later Peter III) were bypassed.
2 CONC  Shrewd and courageous,  Catherine defended Peter's advisers against his rages, and in her own  reign she established, and concentrated power in, the supreme privy  council. Two of her eight children by Peter survived, Anna (mothe
2 CONC r of  Peter III) and Elizabeth Petrovna (empress 1741-62).
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT LATER "CATHERINE/YEKATERINA I"; TSARESS/EMPRESS 1725-1727
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Catherine I of Russia (1683-1727) aka Martha Skavronskaya - daughter of a Lithuanian peasant named Skavronsky who died when she was a child. She became a servant in the home of Pastor Gluck of Marienburg district, and then married
2 CONC  a Swedish dragoon named Johan.  When the Swedes evacuated Marienburg, she became a prisoner of war of Marhsal Sheremetev, who sold her to Prince Menshikov.  At his home Martha became the lover and mistress of Peter the Great.  Sh
2 CONC e and Peter were later married.  She joined the Russian Orthodox church and was baptised Catherine Alexeyevna, the tsarevich Alexius was her Godfather.  After Peter divorced his 1st wife, Eudoxia, they were married in 1711.  She a
2 CONC ccompanied him during his war campaigns, and he attributed his success to her courage and sang-froid. She acted as a buffer between him and his advisors and his rages.  She was proclaimed Peter's successor, and solomenly crowned e
2 CONC mpress-consort in the Upensky cathedral at Moscow on 7 May 1724.  Her husband died a few months later, Jan 28, 1725.  Though illiterate, she was uncommonly shrewd, sensisble, good-tempered, but lashishly extravagant. She died 16 M
2 CONC ay 1727.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT She was of peasant origin and orphaned. She was taken prisoner by a Russian commander and sold to an advisor to Peter, becoming his mistress and most influential counselor. Peter, having divorced his first wife in 1699, married Ca
2 CONC therine in 1712. Catherine was shrewd and courageous; In time, succeeding Peter as Empress of Russia having been crowned Empress-consort 1724. They would have 8 children, with 2 of them surviving: Anna and Elizabeth Petrovna, late
2 CONC r Empress 1741-1762.
2 CONT
