2 CONT Lithuanian peasant named Skavronsky who died when she was a child. She
2 CONT became a servant in the home of Pastor Gluck of Marienburg district, and
2 CONT then married a Swedish dragoon named Johan.  When the Swedes evacuated
2 CONT Marienburg, she became a prisoner of war of Marhsal Sheremetev, who sold
2 CONT her to Prince Menshikov.  At his home Martha became the lover and mistress
2 CONT of Peter the Great.  She and Peter were later married.  She joined the
2 CONT Russian Orthodox church and was baptised Catherine Alexeyevna, the
2 CONT tsarevich Alexius was her Godfather.  After Peter divorced his 1st wife,
2 CONT  Eudoxia, they were married in 1711.  She accompanied him during his war
2 CONT campaigns, and he attributed his success to her courage and sang-froid.
2 CONT She acted as a buffer between him and his advisors and his rages.  She was
2 CONT proclaimed Peter's successor, and solomenly crowned empress-consort in the
2 CONT Upensky cathedral at Moscow on 7 May 1724.  Her husband died a few months
2 CONT later, Jan 28, 1725.  Though illiterate, she was uncommonly shrewd,
2 CONT sensisble, good-tempered, but lashishly extravagant.
2 CONT She died 16 May 1727.
